1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,012
In 2021, Intel sold seventy-nine billion dollars' worth of chips,

2
00:00:05,012 --> 00:00:08,420
and kept almost twenty billion of it as profit.

3
00:00:08,870 --> 00:00:13,870
Three years later, it lost almost nineteen billion dollars in a single year.

4
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:18,951
For half a century, Intel set the pace for the whole chip industry.

5
00:00:18,951 --> 00:00:21,922
It put a computer's brain on a single chip,

6
00:00:21,922 --> 00:00:26,000
and its processors ran most of the world's PCs and servers.

7
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:29,802
Today, a company that only makes chips for others,

8
00:00:29,802 --> 00:00:32,819
TSMC, sells more than twice as much as Intel.

9
00:00:32,819 --> 00:00:35,970
And the US government owns about a tenth of it.

10
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:38,690
How do you lose a crown like that?

11
00:00:38,690 --> 00:00:40,026
Not in one bad year.

12
00:00:40,026 --> 00:00:44,700
It took a string of decisions that each looked reasonable at the time.

13
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:47,990
So let's go back to the beginning, and watch it happen.

14
00:00:51,590 --> 00:00:56,732
Intel was founded in 1968 by Robert Noyce and Gordon Moore,

15
00:00:56,732 --> 00:01:00,566
in what would soon be called Silicon Valley.

16
00:01:00,566 --> 00:01:06,318
In 1971, it released the 4004, which Intel calls the world's first

17
00:01:06,318 --> 00:01:12,070
programmable microprocessor: the brain of a computer, on one chip.

18
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:16,862
But for its first years, Intel was mostly a memory company.

19
00:01:16,862 --> 00:01:21,204
Then Japanese rivals started selling memory chips for less.

20
00:01:21,204 --> 00:01:24,736
In 1985, Intel made what it calls "the difficult

21
00:01:24,736 --> 00:01:27,680
but smart decision" to stop making them.

22
00:01:28,130 --> 00:01:32,079
Andy Grove, Intel's president at the time, later told the story.

23
00:01:32,079 --> 00:01:36,707
He asked Gordon Moore what a new boss would do if the board threw them out.

24
00:01:36,707 --> 00:01:38,806
Moore's answer: get out of memory.

25
00:01:38,806 --> 00:01:40,410
So they did it themselves.

26
00:01:40,860 --> 00:01:43,292
That left the microprocessor.

27
00:01:43,292 --> 00:01:47,737
And Intel already had the best customer in the world.

28
00:01:47,737 --> 00:01:54,027
In 1981, IBM had picked an Intel processor for its first personal computer.

29
00:01:54,027 --> 00:02:00,820
Every PC built to be compatible with IBM's needed a chip compatible with Intel's.

30
00:02:01,270 --> 00:02:04,497
In 1991, Intel launched "Intel Inside",

31
00:02:04,497 --> 00:02:09,710
an advertising campaign for a part most buyers would never see.

32
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:12,533
But the real secret was the factory.

33
00:02:12,533 --> 00:02:15,566
Intel designed its chips and made them itself,

34
00:02:15,566 --> 00:02:19,060
and for decades its factories were the best on earth.

35
00:02:19,060 --> 00:02:24,400
Roughly every two years, it shrank its transistors and left rivals a step behind.

36
00:02:24,850 --> 00:02:30,436
In 2005, even Apple announced it would put Intel chips in its Macs.

37
00:02:30,436 --> 00:02:35,021
In servers, by 2017, its old rival AMD was down to less

38
00:02:35,021 --> 00:02:39,690
than one percent of the market, by its own boss's count.

39
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:42,322
And it showed in the accounts.

40
00:02:42,322 --> 00:02:45,522
As late as 2021, Intel kept about fifty-five

41
00:02:45,522 --> 00:02:48,940
cents of gross profit on every dollar of sales.

42
00:02:49,390 --> 00:02:51,690
But while Intel was winning the desk,

43
00:02:51,690 --> 00:02:55,110
a new computer was about to land in everybody's pocket.

44
00:02:56,210 --> 00:03:01,526
In June 2006, Intel sold its business making processors for phones and

45
00:03:01,526 --> 00:03:07,450
handheld devices to a company called Marvell, for six hundred million dollars.

46
00:03:07,900 --> 00:03:12,100
Seven months later, Steve Jobs unveiled the iPhone.

47
00:03:12,550 --> 00:03:14,809
Intel could have been inside it.

48
00:03:14,809 --> 00:03:19,821
In 2013, its outgoing chief executive, Paul Otellini, told The Atlantic

49
00:03:19,821 --> 00:03:24,691
that Apple wanted a chip at a certain price, "and not a nickel more".

50
00:03:24,691 --> 00:03:29,350
That price was below what Intel expected the chip to cost to make.

51
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:33,682
His words: "I couldn't see it." And then: "In hindsight,

52
00:03:33,682 --> 00:03:38,880
the forecasted cost was wrong and the volume was 100x what anyone thought."

53
00:03:39,330 --> 00:03:42,584
Phones ended up running on someone else's designs.

54
00:03:42,584 --> 00:03:47,269
Arm, a British company that licenses chip blueprints, says its processor

55
00:03:47,269 --> 00:03:52,410
designs are inside more than ninety-nine percent of the smartphones sold today.

56
00:03:52,860 --> 00:03:57,166
Intel did try to catch up, with its low-power Atom chips.

57
00:03:57,166 --> 00:04:01,773
To get them into tablets, it helped pay device makers' costs.

58
00:04:01,773 --> 00:04:05,928
In 2014, its chips went into forty-six million tablets,

59
00:04:05,928 --> 00:04:10,460
and its mobile division lost four point two billion dollars.

60
00:04:10,910 --> 00:04:16,079
In the last three months of that year, the division's revenue was actually negative.

61
00:04:16,079 --> 00:04:18,110
It paid out more than it took in.

62
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:21,964
In 2016, Intel cancelled its next phone chips.

63
00:04:21,964 --> 00:04:25,294
In 2019, it sold most of its smartphone modem

64
00:04:25,294 --> 00:04:28,920
business to Apple, for about one billion dollars.

65
00:04:29,370 --> 00:04:31,973
Why does missing phones matter so much?

66
00:04:31,973 --> 00:04:34,910
Because in chipmaking, volume is everything.

67
00:04:34,910 --> 00:04:38,782
Each new generation of factories costs more than the last,

68
00:04:38,782 --> 00:04:41,519
and the company that makes the most chips

69
00:04:41,519 --> 00:04:44,389
learns fastest and can afford the next one.

70
00:04:44,389 --> 00:04:47,326
Billions of phones handed that volume to the

71
00:04:47,326 --> 00:04:50,730
chipmakers that served them, and above all to TSMC.

72
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:52,655
Intel didn't feel it yet.

73
00:04:52,655 --> 00:04:54,839
PCs and servers still made a fortune.

74
00:04:54,839 --> 00:04:57,140
The bill arrived through its factories.

75
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:03,991
Intel's rhythm had been simple: a new manufacturing process roughly every two years.

76
00:05:03,991 --> 00:05:05,840
Then it hit ten nanometers.

77
00:05:06,290 --> 00:05:12,856
In 2013, Intel said its ten-nanometer chips could reach the market by 2015.

78
00:05:12,856 --> 00:05:17,584
That slipped to 2016, then to the second half of 2017.

79
00:05:17,584 --> 00:05:20,210
Real volume only came in 2019.

80
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:25,028
Meanwhile, TSMC started volume production of its

81
00:05:25,028 --> 00:05:30,580
seven-nanometer process in 2018, and five nanometers in 2020.

82
00:05:31,030 --> 00:05:35,192
One warning: those names no longer measure anything physical.

83
00:05:35,192 --> 00:05:38,739
Intel's ten nanometers roughly matched TSMC's seven,

84
00:05:38,739 --> 00:05:41,264
and Intel later renamed it "Intel 7".

85
00:05:41,264 --> 00:05:46,790
But for the first time, the most advanced factories in the world weren't Intel's.

86
00:05:47,240 --> 00:05:52,458
Then, in July 2020, Intel admitted that its next process, seven nanometers,

87
00:05:52,458 --> 00:05:56,633
was trending about twelve months behind its internal target.

88
00:05:56,633 --> 00:06:00,112
The first products would slip by about six months.

89
00:06:00,112 --> 00:06:03,800
The next day, its shares closed down sixteen percent.

90
00:06:04,250 --> 00:06:08,895
That same summer, Apple announced it would stop using Intel processors

91
00:06:08,895 --> 00:06:13,275
in its Macs, and switch to chips of its own design, built by TSMC.

92
00:06:13,275 --> 00:06:15,930
Fifteen years after it had joined Intel.

93
00:06:16,380 --> 00:06:17,137
And AMD?

94
00:06:17,137 --> 00:06:21,582
It had spun off its own factories back in 2009.

95
00:06:21,582 --> 00:06:26,500
So when TSMC took the lead, AMD's chips took it too.

96
00:06:26,950 --> 00:06:32,061
By early 2026, according to the research firm Mercury Research,

97
00:06:32,061 --> 00:06:38,307
AMD earned forty-six percent of all the money spent on x86 server processors.

98
00:06:38,307 --> 00:06:41,390
Up from less than one percent in 2017.

99
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:43,556
This was the tipping point.

100
00:06:43,556 --> 00:06:48,578
Intel's great advantage, making its own chips, had become its biggest weakness.

101
00:06:48,578 --> 00:06:53,282
When its factories fell behind, every Intel product fell behind with them.

102
00:06:53,282 --> 00:06:57,160
Its rivals could simply rent the best factories in the world.

103
00:06:58,260 --> 00:07:00,380
And then came the biggest wave of all.

104
00:07:00,830 --> 00:07:04,422
In 2005, according to The New York Times, Paul Otellini

105
00:07:04,422 --> 00:07:08,470
proposed buying Nvidia, for as much as twenty billion dollars.

106
00:07:08,470 --> 00:07:11,474
The board pushed back, and Intel built its own

107
00:07:11,474 --> 00:07:14,870
graphics chip instead, a project it later cancelled.

108
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:20,218
Around 2017, Reuters reported, Intel discussed buying fifteen

109
00:07:20,218 --> 00:07:25,116
percent of a small lab called OpenAI for one billion dollars.

110
00:07:25,116 --> 00:07:26,320
It walked away.

111
00:07:26,770 --> 00:07:29,404
When artificial intelligence took off,

112
00:07:29,404 --> 00:07:33,147
the chips everyone wanted were Nvidia's, made by TSMC.

113
00:07:33,147 --> 00:07:35,850
We told that story in our Nvidia video.

114
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:39,969
Intel's own AI chip, called Gaudi, had a modest goal:

115
00:07:39,969 --> 00:07:43,154
five hundred million dollars of sales in 2024.

116
00:07:43,154 --> 00:07:47,100
In October of that year, Intel said it wouldn't reach it.

117
00:07:47,550 --> 00:07:50,937
So by the early 2020s, Intel had missed phones,

118
00:07:50,937 --> 00:07:54,684
lost its manufacturing lead, and missed the AI boom.

119
00:07:54,684 --> 00:07:57,350
Someone had to try something drastic.

120
00:07:58,450 --> 00:08:01,372
In 2021, Intel brought back Pat Gelsinger,

121
00:08:01,372 --> 00:08:05,130
an engineer who had spent thirty years at the company.

122
00:08:05,580 --> 00:08:07,662
His plan was called IDM 2.0.

123
00:08:07,662 --> 00:08:11,306
Win back the manufacturing lead, and open Intel's

124
00:08:11,306 --> 00:08:15,248
factories to other chip companies, the way TSMC does.

125
00:08:15,248 --> 00:08:20,602
It started with twenty billion dollars for two new factories in Arizona.

126
00:08:20,602 --> 00:08:22,759
Then came a new site in Ohio,

127
00:08:22,759 --> 00:08:26,849
about twenty-eight billion dollars for its first phase.

128
00:08:26,849 --> 00:08:31,460
And a promise: five new manufacturing processes in four years.

129
00:08:31,910 --> 00:08:35,011
Washington wanted more chips made at home, too.

130
00:08:35,011 --> 00:08:39,367
Under the CHIPS Act, Intel's award was finalized in November 2024:

131
00:08:39,367 --> 00:08:42,270
up to seven point eight six billion dollars.

132
00:08:42,720 --> 00:08:44,440
But the bill came first.

133
00:08:44,890 --> 00:08:49,286
Intel's factory business, now called Intel Foundry,

134
00:08:49,286 --> 00:08:52,303
lost seven billion dollars in 2023.

135
00:08:52,303 --> 00:08:55,923
Then thirteen point three billion in 2024.

136
00:08:55,923 --> 00:08:59,113
Then ten point three billion in 2025.

137
00:08:59,113 --> 00:09:03,250
More than thirty billion dollars in three years.

138
00:09:03,700 --> 00:09:06,543
And the rest of Intel was selling less.

139
00:09:06,543 --> 00:09:09,897
Revenue fell from seventy-nine billion dollars

140
00:09:09,897 --> 00:09:12,740
in 2021 to fifty-three billion in 2024.

141
00:09:13,190 --> 00:09:17,853
In August 2024, Intel announced it would cut fifteen

142
00:09:17,853 --> 00:09:22,605
percent of its workforce, and suspended its dividend.

143
00:09:22,605 --> 00:09:29,510
The next day, its shares fell twenty-six percent, their worst day since 1974.

144
00:09:29,960 --> 00:09:35,145
That year, Intel lost eighteen point eight billion dollars.

145
00:09:35,145 --> 00:09:42,440
Its shares lost about sixty percent, their worst year since it went public in 1971.

146
00:09:42,890 --> 00:09:45,659
In December 2024, Gelsinger left.

147
00:09:45,659 --> 00:09:48,092
Intel called it a retirement.

148
00:09:48,092 --> 00:09:54,973
Bloomberg reported that the board had forced him out, frustrated by slow progress.

149
00:09:54,973 --> 00:10:01,770
In February 2025, the first Ohio factory was pushed back to 2030 at the earliest.

150
00:10:02,870 --> 00:10:07,814
In March 2025, Intel named a new chief executive, Lip-Bu Tan,

151
00:10:07,814 --> 00:10:12,110
a chip industry veteran who had sat on Intel's board.

152
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:13,419
He cut hard.

153
00:10:13,419 --> 00:10:18,787
Intel went from about one hundred and nine thousand employees at the end of

154
00:10:18,787 --> 00:10:24,371
2024 to about eighty-five thousand a year later, partly by selling businesses.

155
00:10:24,371 --> 00:10:28,307
One of them was Altera, a chip company Intel had bought

156
00:10:28,307 --> 00:10:31,743
in 2015 for sixteen point seven billion dollars.

157
00:10:31,743 --> 00:10:37,040
It sold a majority stake at a valuation of eight point seven five billion.

158
00:10:37,490 --> 00:10:39,890
Then money arrived from unusual places.

159
00:10:40,340 --> 00:10:45,380
In August 2025, SoftBank agreed to invest two billion dollars.

160
00:10:45,830 --> 00:10:50,117
Days later, the US government agreed to take a nine point nine

161
00:10:50,117 --> 00:10:53,713
percent stake, for eight point nine billion dollars,

162
00:10:53,713 --> 00:10:58,830
paid mostly with CHIPS Act grants that had already been promised to Intel.

163
00:10:59,280 --> 00:11:03,285
And in September, the company that had taken the AI crown, Nvidia,

164
00:11:03,285 --> 00:11:08,200
agreed to invest five billion dollars, and to build products together with Intel.

165
00:11:08,650 --> 00:11:12,400
The factories finally had some good news, too.

166
00:11:12,400 --> 00:11:17,780
In October 2025, Intel said its newest Arizona factory was running

167
00:11:17,780 --> 00:11:22,671
its 18A process, which it calls the most advanced chipmaking

168
00:11:22,671 --> 00:11:27,480
technology developed and manufactured in the United States.

169
00:11:27,480 --> 00:11:32,290
The first laptops built on it went on sale in January 2026.

170
00:11:32,740 --> 00:11:35,657
The missing piece is outside customers.

171
00:11:35,657 --> 00:11:40,594
In May 2026, The Wall Street Journal reported that Apple and Intel

172
00:11:40,594 --> 00:11:45,530
had reached a preliminary deal for Intel to make some Apple chips.

173
00:11:45,530 --> 00:11:49,868
In June, President Trump said Apple would work with Intel.

174
00:11:49,868 --> 00:11:52,860
Neither company has published any terms.

175
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:55,560
So, where does that leave Intel?

176
00:11:56,010 --> 00:11:59,610
In 2025, its revenue was fifty-two point nine

177
00:11:59,610 --> 00:12:02,890
billion dollars, flat on the year before.

178
00:12:02,890 --> 00:12:06,570
TSMC's was one hundred and twenty-two billion,

179
00:12:06,570 --> 00:12:10,090
with a gross margin of almost sixty percent.

180
00:12:10,090 --> 00:12:12,490
Intel's was under thirty-five.

181
00:12:12,490 --> 00:12:15,050
The margins have swapped places.

182
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:17,172
But 2026 has been kinder.

183
00:12:17,172 --> 00:12:21,721
Between July and September, Intel's revenue grew twenty-one percent,

184
00:12:21,721 --> 00:12:24,263
to sixteen point five billion dollars.

185
00:12:24,263 --> 00:12:27,741
Its data center business grew seventy-eight percent,

186
00:12:27,741 --> 00:12:32,089
and Intel made a profit again: three point three billion dollars.

187
00:12:32,089 --> 00:12:37,507
The AI boom, it turns out, also needs ordinary processors alongside the AI chips.

188
00:12:37,507 --> 00:12:40,451
Yet in the same quarter, Intel Foundry still

189
00:12:40,451 --> 00:12:43,260
lost one point eight five billion dollars.

190
00:12:43,710 --> 00:12:45,910
So how did Intel lose the chip crown?

191
00:12:46,360 --> 00:12:48,264
Not through one mistake.

192
00:12:48,264 --> 00:12:54,770
It passed on the phone, and with it, the biggest source of chip volume in history.

193
00:12:54,770 --> 00:12:58,816
Its factories slipped, just as TSMC's pulled ahead.

194
00:12:58,816 --> 00:13:01,752
Its rivals simply rented TSMC's lead.

195
00:13:01,752 --> 00:13:05,560
And the AI boom arrived on someone else's chips.

196
00:13:06,010 --> 00:13:09,693
What made Intel great, designing and making its own chips,

197
00:13:09,693 --> 00:13:12,170
is exactly what made the fall so steep.

198
00:13:12,620 --> 00:13:15,231
Its comeback now rests on one question:

199
00:13:15,231 --> 00:13:18,779
will other companies trust Intel to make their chips?

200
00:13:18,779 --> 00:13:21,523
Investors have already placed their bets.

201
00:13:21,523 --> 00:13:26,209
In 2026, Intel's share price passed its old record from the year 2000.

202
00:13:26,209 --> 00:13:30,895
But in chips, the crown doesn't go to the company with the best story.

203
00:13:30,895 --> 00:13:34,176
It goes to whoever makes the most advanced chips,

204
00:13:34,176 --> 00:13:37,054
in the biggest volumes, at the lowest cost.

205
00:13:37,054 --> 00:13:38,460
For now, that's TSMC.

206
00:13:38,910 --> 00:13:40,881
And the lesson goes beyond Intel.

207
00:13:40,881 --> 00:13:45,182
In an industry that runs on a cost curve, losing the volume comes first.

208
00:13:45,182 --> 00:13:47,750
Losing the lead just takes a little longer.
